Patch 6503 (R&C pass 3): each raw SPU runs the image that reached its own local store.

raw_spu_start kept the resolved lifted entry in one file-scope variable (s_pending_entry) that was never cleared,
and the proxy-GET lookup only ran while it was empty. Tools of Destruction streams igcollspu into raw SPU 0 and then
igCollBroadSpu into raw SPU 1: spu1 skipped its own lookup and ran spu0's image (tods-ng-lua: no proxy-loaded or
window line for spu1, "spu1 START ... image=40", "[spu] img=40 exit: synthesised stop 0x0000 at LS 0x00000" after
1 step). The image choice is now per SPU (spu_raw_resolve.h, pure, host-testable): proxy GET runs since the SPU's
last start (and the declared windows of patch 6502), else the image sys_raw_spu_image_load bound to that SPU (the
import before the load), else the image the SPU already ran (a restart: its LS still holds it; logged when GETs
since the last start matched nothing), else, only if nothing was streamed, the latest import. A stream that matches
nothing never runs another SPU's image. sys_raw_spu_create clears the SPU's state. Log lines are unchanged.
Test: runtime/spu/tests/test_spu_raw_two_spus.c (host, CI linux gcc/clang + macos). General; upstream candidate.
Rebased onto v0.18.0 + KZ3 2c5081a5, 2026-11-02, shrunk: upstream PR #473 (2e97cd5e) fixes the ToD bug itself (the
import is consumed by the next start; a restart with nothing streamed keeps the SPU's image). What this still adds:
streams take priority over a pending import; sys_raw_spu_image_load binds the import to its SPU; a restart after
GETs that matched nothing keeps the current image (upstream refuses to run); an unmatched stream never falls back to
the import. An import that reaches a start directly is consumed by that start (upstream's rule); one bound by
image_load is not. spu_raw_resolve.h now takes the masked lookup (optional), npc (logged as upstream does) and the
SPU_RAW_DUMP_DIR dump. Upstream's runtime/spu/tests/test_spu_raw_start.c checks s_import.entry instead of
s_pending_entry; test_spu_raw_two_spus.c adds the import consumption and a masked program.

